@startuml 06-grading-task-state
' =====================================================================
' grading_task lifecycle
' Traces to: FR-18, FR-29, FR-30, FR-42, NFR-6
'
' One row per (submission, grading target). Two fields:
'   ai_needed  -- does this target need AI feedback at all
'   ai_status  -- has that feedback been produced
'
' ai_needed is the important one. It is set by the submission
' autograder after functional testing, under instructor control, and it
' is what stops the system spending tokens explaining correct code.
' =====================================================================

title grading_task -- ai_needed and ai_status

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica

state "Resolving ai_needed\n(AI phase)" as Marking

[*] --> Marking : the test suite has exited;\nthe AI phase creates one task\nper REGISTERED target

Marking : Apply the target's ai_feedback_policy.
Marking : NEVER or ALWAYS are decided by config alone.
Marking : AUTOGRADER defers to the test suite: did it
Marking : call request_ai_feedback for this target?

Marking --> NOT_NEEDED : NEVER\n(reason POLICY_NEVER)
Marking --> NOT_NEEDED : AUTOGRADER and the suite\nnever requested it\n(reason NOT_REQUESTED)
Marking --> NEEDED : ALWAYS\n(reason POLICY_ALWAYS)
Marking --> NEEDED : AUTOGRADER and the suite\nrequested it\n(reason AUTOGRADER_REQUEST)

note as N_NN #FFF8DC
  Marking is the instructor's only lever, and it
  happens here, during the submission autograder
  run. There is no post-hoc "grade this after
  all" path: once a submission is AI-graded it
  is done, and the nightly trigger never selects
  it again.
end note

NOT_NEEDED : ai_needed = false.
NOT_NEEDED : The student's code satisfied this
NOT_NEEDED : target, or the instructor excluded
NOT_NEEDED : it. No tokens spent.

NEEDED : ai_needed = true, ai_status = PENDING.
NEEDED : **This is the work queue.** The nightly
NEEDED : trigger queries it to decide which
NEEDED : submissions to rerun.

NEEDED --> DONE : AI phase generated feedback.\nDuring the submission run if the\ntarget is IMMEDIATE, on a\ntriggered rerun if NIGHTLY
NEEDED --> NEEDED : attempt failed,\nattempt_count < 3
NEEDED --> GAVE_UP : attempt_count reached 3

DONE : ai_status = DONE, completed_at set.
DONE : Terminal. Once no task on the submission
DONE : is still NEEDED, the submission's
DONE : ai_graded_submitted_at is set and the
DONE : nightly trigger stops selecting it.

GAVE_UP : Repeated failures. Surfaced on the
GAVE_UP : instructor dashboard. Counts as "not
GAVE_UP : NEEDED" for the guard, so one broken
GAVE_UP : target cannot hold a submission in the
GAVE_UP : nightly queue forever.

DONE --> [*]
NOT_NEEDED --> [*]
GAVE_UP --> [*]

note as N_MARK #FFF8DC
Config and code, chosen per target. NEVER and
  ALWAYS are decided by the dropdown alone and
  need no programming. AUTOGRADER delegates the
  per-submission decision to the instructor's
  test suite, which calls request_ai_feedback
  for the targets it wants graded.

  AUTOGRADER is the default because the usual
  rule, "explain it when the tests fail", is one
  line of the instructor's own code and can be
  refined to any condition she likes without
  touching this system.
end note

note as N1 #FFF8DC
  **Why NEEDED is not a scheduler state**
  Rerun is per submission, so exactly one
  container works on a submission at a time.
  There is nothing to race against, so no claim
  step and no IN_PROGRESS state are required.

  Retry falls out of the design: a task that
  fails stays NEEDED, the submission guard is
  not set, and the next nightly trigger picks
  the submission up and retries only the tasks
  still marked NEEDED. attempt_count exists
  solely to stop a permanently broken target
  being retried every night forever.

  The same mechanism carries mixed-mode
  submissions. After the IMMEDIATE pass, NIGHTLY
  tasks are still NEEDED, so the submission
  enters the queue with no extra flag, and the
  IMMEDIATE tasks are skipped on the rerun
  because they are already DONE.
end note

note as N2 #FFF8DC
  **Two levels, two jobs**
  grading_task.ai_needed decides WHETHER a
  target deserves feedback. It is the token
  saving, and the instructor controls it through
  ai_feedback_policy and, under AUTOGRADER,
  through her own test suite.

  submission.ai_graded_submitted_at decides
  whether this submission is finished. The
  nightly trigger filters on it, so a fully
  graded submission is never rerun. The AI phase
  re-checks it, which catches reruns the trigger
  did not cause, such as an instructor debugging
  the autograder.

  Set the guard when no task is still NEEDED,
  counting GAVE_UP as settled. Setting it while
  a task is still NEEDED would strand that
  target ungraded.
end note

@enduml
